1. The central mathematical object
Start with a fixed underlying state space:
\[
\Omega = \{\text{all possible configurations}\}
\]

At time \(t\), the system has three structures:
\[
\mathcal S_t=(z_t,\sim_t,\mathcal R_t)
\]

Where:
- \(z_t\in\Omega\): the system's current configuration.
- \(\sim_t\): its current state-distinguishability relation.
- \(\mathcal R_t\): its currently admissible transition relation.
The important addition is \(\sim_t\).

Two configurations may be considered equivalent at one moment and distinguishable later:
\[
z_i\sim_t z_j
\]

but
\[
z_i\not\sim_{t+1}z_j
\]

This means the system has changed its own representation of what constitutes a different state.
2. How the mathematics operates
The system has three coupled operations.

A. Determine the current distinctions

An equivalence relation partitions the underlying state space into classes:
\[
Q_t=\Omega/\sim_t
\]

For example, several different configurations might currently belong to the same class because the system treats them as equivalent.

B. Determine which transitions are permitted

Define:
\[
\mathcal R_t\subseteq Q_t\times Q_t
\]

A transition is permitted only if it belongs to this relation:
\[
q_t\rightarrow q_{t+1}
\]

The transition rules therefore operate on the system's current distinctions, rather than necessarily on every underlying configuration separately.

C. Modify the distinctions

After a transition, a meta-update changes the equivalence relation:
\[
\sim_{t+1}
=
M(\sim_t,z_t,z_{t+1},\mathcal R_t)
\]

The admissible transition relation can also change:
\[
\mathcal R_{t+1}
=
G(\mathcal R_t,\sim_t,\sim_{t+1},z_{t+1})
\]

The complete evolution is consequently:
\[
\boxed{
(z_t,\sim_t,\mathcal R_t)
\longrightarrow
(z_{t+1},\sim_{t+1},\mathcal R_{t+1})
}
\]

This is the basic mathematical proposal.
3. What makes this different from ordinary self-modifying systems?
Consider a system that initially treats three configurations as equivalent:
\[
\{A,B,C\}
\]

It has one available transition category.

After observing a particular transition, it discovers that \(C\) behaves differently. Its classification changes:
\[
\{A,B,C\}
\longrightarrow
\{A,B\},\{C\}
\]

That change can alter the transition structure.

Previously:
\[
[A,B,C]\rightarrow[D]
\]

Afterward:
\[
[A,B]\rightarrow[D]
\]

\[
[C]\rightarrow[E]
\]

The system has not merely changed a numerical parameter. It has changed the classification of its own possible states, and that classification changes the structure of its permitted evolution.

That is the property worth investigating.
4. The mathematical difficulty we must solve
There is a serious issue.

If we simply define a larger state containing the configuration, equivalence relation, and rules, then the entire system can be represented as an ordinary dynamical system:
\[
\mathcal S_{t+1}=F(\mathcal S_t)
\]

That observation alone does not establish new mathematics.

The actual research question is whether the coupled structure has a property that existing frameworks do not already capture, or whether it is a useful new formulation of existing mathematics.

One particularly important technical issue is consistency under changing equivalence relations.

Suppose:
\[
z_1\sim_t z_2
\]

If the system treats these configurations as equivalent, their permitted transitions must be compatible with that equivalence. Otherwise, the quotient transition system is not well-defined.

A useful consistency condition is:
\[
z_1\sim_t z_2
\implies
\operatorname{Succ}_t(z_1)
\sim_t
\operatorname{Succ}_t(z_2)
\]

Here, the implication means that equivalent configurations must have compatible successor classes. The exact condition will depend on whether transitions are deterministic or nondeterministic.

This is where the research becomes technically meaningful: the system must be able to change its distinctions without making its transition mathematics incoherent.
